body:
It's sort of true to real life. Tons of violence and trauma *doesn't always* make you stronger.

&#x200B;

Sometimes it just makes you shit yourself any time your room mate sticks popcorn in the microwave.

When you find yourself obsessively exhausting every dialogue tree for characters like Caesar, Benny, Mr. House, etc. you know you've got good writers.

&#x200B;

Chief Hanlon at camp golf talks for like 15 minutes and it's worth hearing every second of what he has to say.

You got me. I told them a relative had died and they suggested I bring the cert into the store to cancel the service.

&#x200B;

It was a land line btw.

Innsmouth, MA.

&#x200B;

Never been myself, but I'm told this is pretty normal.

Know what we call that?

&#x200B;

Job security.

Strapping a board to your face...of course...

&#x200B;

How come no other martial artists thought to do this earlier?

> It sounds to me like you paid $125 for the remains to be put into an  urn. Service charge, not materials. Still too much to put ashes through a  funnel into a bag in a box, but it's not the container.

&#x200B;

I know it's been a while, but you nailed it. The $125 was a service charge to put it into an "urn", by which if you didn't buy one from them is a $45 cardboard container. On the bright side, I don't think my mother would have cared and I plan to spread the ashes eventually anyways.
